lumn from both tables. Both tables probably have an `id`, and `user_id` and the like appear twice, so clients that key results by column name can get collisions. List the columns you need, for example `o.id, o.total, o.created_at, u.email`.\n\n2. **`lower(u.email) = '...'`**: Wrapping the column in a function stops a plain index on `users.email` from being used, so the query scans the whole table. There are three ways to fix it:\n   - Store emails already lowercased and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'`.\n   - Add an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`\n   - In Postgres, use the `citext` type.\n\n3. **Indexes**: Make sure `orders.user_id` is indexed (it often isn't, since only the primary key gets one automatically). Without that index, the join has to scan `orders`.\n\n4. **Join shape**: If you only need the orders for one user, you can use a subquery or CTE to find the user first. Most planners handle the join fine, so this is mainly about readability.\n\nA cleaned-up version:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.total, o.created_at, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE u.email = 'a@example.org';  -- assumes emails are stored normalized to lowercase\n```\n\nIf the query is parameterized in your application, use a bind parameter instead of the literal. That avoids injection risk and lets the plan be reused.\n\nI don't know your schema, columns or database engine, so the column names and the index syntax above are guesses. Tell me which engine you use and what you need from the result, and I can make it more exact. I can also edit the file if you like.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}